('171','STI-related Genital Warts','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','452','452-452','yy Usually no symptoms yy Small painless swellings in the ano-genital region yy Itching or discomfort in the genital area yy May cause increased vaginal discharge yy Anal or vaginal bleeding during or after sex','yy Small, flat, papular, pedunculated, flesh-coloured swellings on the skin and mucous membranes of the genitals (penis, vulva, vagina, cervix, urethra, perianal region)','yy No investigations required, in view of the syndromic approach yy Recommended in managing STIs','Treatment objectives yy To relieve pain yy To relieve the swelling yy To treat the infection of lymphogranuloma venereum and chancroid concurrently Non-pharmacological treatment yy Aspiration of fluctuant buboes using a wide bore needle through adjacent healthy skin every second or third day. An incision and drainage should not be attempted. If buboes persist, the patient should be referred. yy Sequelae such as strictures and/or fistula may require surgery. Pharmacological treatment A. For Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV) and Chancroid Evidence Rating: [C] yy Doxycycline, oral, 100 mg 12 hourly for 21 days Or yy Azithromycin, oral, 1 g stat. Or yy Erythromycin, oral, 500 mg 6 hourly for 14 days 171. STI-related Genital Warts Causes yy Human papilloma virus Symptoms yy Usually no symptoms yy Small painless swellings in the ano-genital region yy Itching or discomfort in the genital area yy May cause increased vaginal discharge yy Anal or vaginal bleeding during or after sex Signs yy Small, flat, papular, pedunculated, flesh-coloured swellings on the skin and mucous membranes of the genitals (penis, vulva, vagina, cervix, urethra, perianal region)',NULL,'— STI-related Genital Warts  —
