182.	Fever  
Fever is a common complaint, which is usually related to an infection 
of viral, bacterial or parasitic origin. It may be a valuable guide to the 
diagnosis and severity of infections.
Fever is defined as an axillary temperature above 37.5 °C (read after 
keeping the thermometer in place for 3 minutes).  Fever above 38 °C in 
children and adults often needs urgent attention, especially if the patient 
is restless or delirious. Not every fever is due to malaria or typhoid. 
Every fever should be investigated and treated appropriately. A thorough 
history, physical examination and appropriate investigation would usually 
reveal the cause of the fever.
In neonates and the elderly, severe infections may not be accompanied 
by a fever. In infants and young children, fever may be associated with 
convulsions, collapse or coma. (See table below for possible differential 
diagnoses and appropriate action) 
