E.     Intermittent Preventive Treatment in Pregnancy (IPTp)
IPTp consists of giving the fixed-dose combination medication 
Sulphadoxine Pyrimethamine (SP) in treatment doses at predefined 
intervals after quickening (16 gestational weeks). Current recommendation 
is that IPTp with sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine (IPTp-SP) be given to all 
pregnant women at each scheduled antenatal care visit except during the 
first trimester. WHO recommends a schedule of four focused antenatal 
care visits for normal pregnancy. In Ghana, the national malaria control 
strategy reserves SP for the purpose of intermittent preventive treatment 
only. 
To prevent the development of drug resistance, SP is not to be used 
for other purposes such as treatment of acute attacks of malaria. 
