('199','Exposure Keratopathy','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','511','511-511','yy Feeling of drying of the eye yy Foreign body sensations in the eye yy Photophobia yy Blurring of vision','yy Lagophthalmos (inability to close the eyes) yy Incomplete blink (the upper eyelid does not cover the whole cornea during blink) yy Infrequent blink yy Superficial punctate stains on the cornea yy Large coalescent corneal epithelial defect yy Corneal ulceration yy Corneal perforation','yy Fasting blood sugar yy FBC','Treatment objectives yy To improve vision yy To prevent the development of amblyopia in the child Non-pharmacological treatment yy Use of spectacles yy Surgical removal of cataract Pharmacological treatment yy Nil','Refer all cases to the eye specialist. 199. Exposure Keratopathy Exposure keratopathy is the drying of the cornea as a result of inability to close the eyelids or blink adequately. If not detected and treated can result in corneal ulceration and perforation leading to blindness. Causes yy Facial nerve palsy e.g. Bell’s palsy or leprosy yy Scarring of the eyelids yy Coma yy Thyroid eye disease yy Parkinson’s disease yy Proptosis yy Steven Johnson’s Syndrome Symptoms yy Feeling of drying of the eye yy Foreign body sensations in the eye yy Photophobia yy Blurring of vision Signs yy Lagophthalmos (inability to close the eyes) yy Incomplete blink (the upper eyelid does not cover the whole cornea during blink) yy Infrequent blink yy Superficial punctate stains on the cornea yy Large coalescent corneal epithelial defect yy Corneal ulceration yy Corneal perforation','— Exposure Keratopathy —
