('200','Strabismus','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','512','512-512',NULL,NULL,'yy Nil','Treatment objectives yy To moisten the cornea artificially yy To prevent the complications of dry eyes Non-pharmacological treatment yy Taping the eyelids closed in the comatose patient yy Partial tarsorrhaphy yy Education of patients with Parkinson’s or thyroid eye disease to to blink frequently voluntarily rather than rely on reflex blink Pharmacological treatment 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [C] yy Hydroxymethyl cellulose, 0.3% eye drops, 1-2 hourly during waking time 2nd Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [C] yy Polyvinyl alcohol, 1.4-2% eye drops, 1-2 hourly during waking time And yy Chloramphenicol, 1% eye ointment, at night before bedtime Or yy Tetracycline, 1% eye ointment, at bedtime','Refer patients whose exposure keratopathy is not improving to the eye specialist. 200. Strabismus Strabismus is misalignment usually of one eye preventing simultaneous viewing of an object by both eyes. Onset in children under 7 years interferes with the development of the visual system of the deviating eye in the brain leading to amblyopia. Acute onset at an older age causes double vision. Diplopia does not occur in children because of the ability to suppress the second image or development of amblyopia. Amblyopia does not occur if the deviation alternated from one eye to the other. The more frequent types are horizontal misalignment. Esotropia is convergent deviation and exotropia is divergent deviation. Causes yy Congenital misalignment yy Acquired deviation yy Paralysis of the cranial nerve VI, III or IV','— Strabismus —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
512
