ds to run an extremely rapid course (4-6 hours) to respiratory failure and death. It is more common in children. However, the incidence has reduced significantly due to the current immunisation schedule with the pentavalent vaccine. Examination of the throat in patients with this condition must be done only in the presence of a doctor capable and ready to intubate. Causes yy Haemophilus influenzae type B yy Streptococcus pyogenes yy Streptococcus pneumoniae yy Staphylococcus aureus Symptoms yy Sudden onset of high fever yy Drooling of saliva yy Dysphagia yy Breathing difficulty Signs yy Extremely ill and toxic child yy Fever yy Head is held forward to extend the neck yy Breathing difficulty yy Weak voice (not hoarse) yy Reduced air entry on auscultation yy Stridor','— Acute Epiglottitis —
