('210','Epistaxis','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','532','532-533','yy Nose bleed','yy Nose bleed — Epistaxis — Chapter 20: Ear, Nose and Throat Disorders 533 yy Signs related to underlying cause yy Signs of shock (if severe)','yy Ear swab for culture and sensitivity','Treatment objectives yy To keep the ear dry yy To treat any acute exacerbations and complications e.g. mastoiditis Non-pharmacological treatment yy Roll a piece of clean absorbent gauze into a wick and insert carefully into the ear. Leave for one minute then remove and replace with a clean wick. Do frequently (at least 4 times a day) yy If bleeding occurs, drying the ear should be stopped temporarily yy Nothing should be left in the ear between wicking yy Avoid swimming or getting the inside of the ear wet yy Re-assess weekly to ensure that the mother is drying the ear correctly Pharmacological treatment A. For acute exacebations (See section on Treatment for ‘Acute Otitis Media’) B. For topical antibiotic therapy Evidence Rating: [B] yy Gentamicin ear drops, 0.3%, 2-3 drops 6-8 hourly and at night Or yy Ciprofloxacin eye/ear drops, 0.3%, 1 drop 6 hourly daily','Refer all chronically discharging ears to the ENT Specialist. 210. Epistaxis Epistaxis also called nosebleed is a common medical emergency, which requires prompt management to avoid morbidity and mortality. Causes yy Picking of the nose, especially when there is an upper respiratory tract infection yy Trauma yy Sinonasal and nasopharyngeal neoplasms yy Hypertension yy Bleeding disorders yy Atrophic rhinitis Symptoms yy Nose bleed','— Epistaxis —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
532
Investigations
yy
Ear swab for culture and sensitivity
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To keep the ear dry
yy
To treat any acute exacerbations and complications e.g. mastoiditis
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Roll a piece of clean absorbent gauze into a wick and insert carefully 
into the ear. Leave for one minute then remove and replace with a 
clean wick. Do frequently (at least 4 times a day)
yy
If bleeding occurs, drying the ear should be stopped temporarily
yy
Nothing should be left in the ear between wicking 
yy
Avoid swimming or getting the inside of the ear wet
yy
Re-assess weekly to ensure that the mother is drying the ear correctly
Pharmacological treatment 
A.      For acute exacebations 
(See section on Treatment for ‘Acute Otitis Media’)
B.      For topical antibiotic therapy
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Gentamicin ear drops, 0.3%, 2-3 drops 6-8 hourly and at night
Or
yy
Ciprofloxacin eye/ear drops, 0.3%, 1 drop 6 hourly daily
Referral Criteria 
Refer all chronically discharging ears to the ENT Specialist.
