225.	Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis   
Juvenile idiopathic arthritis results from abnormally regulated 
immune responses that lead to inflammation of joints, surrounding 
tissues and other organs. Often, non-rheumatic diseases that can cause 
similar features need to be excluded during evaluation. 
Rheumatoid arthritis in children may present in one of three forms; 
a systemic onset arthritis (Still’s disease), poly-articular onset arthritis (5 
or more joints usually of both large and small affected) or pauci-articular 
onset arthritis (fewer than 5 of mainly large joints affected) either of 
which may be rheumatoid factor positive or negative.  Those with positive 

